// us.irc.temporal — shared amendment anchors for the 119th Congress amending
// Title 26 (DECISION-0379, owner's task Part B). This package owns NO
// section of Title 26 itself: no owner's 2024-Edition base text is pinned
// here (that is each owner's own `sources.law`, generated by
// `corpus/tools/sources/sync/sync_us_irc_fragments.py --package <leaf>`).
// What THIS package pins is the AMENDING Public Law text — the sentences an
// owner's `revision` (§32) cites when the base 2024 Edition text has since
// been amended — so that every owner points at the SAME anchor for the SAME
// enacted amendment, instead of each re-pinning (or re-transcribing) the
// same Public Law bytes independently.
//
// SOURCE AND GENERATION. `sources.law` in this package is GENERATED by
// `corpus/tools/sources/sync/sync_us_irc_public_laws.py --public-law <N>`
// (or `--all` once more Public Laws of `SOURCE-MANIFEST.json.amendments`
// move from `pending` to `obtained`) from the raw USLM XML in
// `docs/foundations/us-irc/raw/plaw/*.xml` (gitignored; 2.8 MB for PL
// 119-21 alone — too large to check in, so a text PROJECTION is pinned
// instead, exactly as `sources/us-irc/<leaf>.txt` projects each owner's
// slice of the whole-title HTML snapshot). The generator extracts ONLY the
// top-level Public Law SECTIONS that `AMENDMENTS.json` records as amending
// Title 26 (`title26_section` set), never the whole bill (most of a
// reconciliation act is not tax law). A handful of PL 119-21 sections that
// themselves insert a whole NEW, separately-tagged `<section>` element
// (e.g. a section literally enacting a new IRC section as nested USLM
// markup, not just quoted text) are not extracted by this generator's
// simple non-nesting heuristic and are reported by name on `--check`/run
// (14 of 104 for PL 119-21) — a documented extraction limitation, not a
// silent gap: `docs/foundations/us-irc/model/anomalies.json` records the
// analogous convention for the base-text extractor.
//
// FRAGMENT ID CONVENTION. `PL<num>_S<sec>` where `<num>` is the Public
// Law number with `-` replaced by `_` (`119-21` → `119_21`, matching
// `sync_us_irc_fragments.py`'s package-name convention for a hyphenated
// path segment) and `<sec>` is the enacting bill section's own number
// (e.g. `PL119_21_S70524`); locator `pl/<num>/section/<sec>`.
//
// HOW AN OWNER CITES THIS PACKAGE. An owner whose section was amended
// writes a `revision` (grammar.ebnf `revision_decl`, §32) in ITS OWN
// package, anchored with `@source(us.irc.temporal::PL<num>_S<sec>)` on the
// revision or on the amendment operation, naming `effective` (the
// applicability date this package's `TEMPORAL.json` also records) and the
// `caused_by`/`relation` to the enacting Public Law. THIS package does not
// write owners' revisions itself (chapter 79's own §7701/§7703 are NOT
// touched by PL 119-21 — see `docs/foundations/us-irc/coverage/definitions.json`
// note and `README.md` in this directory for the worked example this task
// asked for in that case) — see `README.md` for why `us.irc.definitions`
// does not carry a `revision` for PL 119-21 at this stage even though two
// OTHER sections it owns (§7704(d), §7705) were amended by it.
//
// TEMPORAL.json (`docs/foundations/us-irc/TEMPORAL.json`) is the REGISTRY
// row-per-amendment-entry counterpart of this package's fragments: one row
// per `AMENDMENTS.json` entry for PL 119-21, naming the owner package(s),
// the enactment/applicability dates and the fragment id here. This package
// and that registry are generated/maintained together but are not the same
// artifact: the registry is queryable data (schema-checked JSON), this
// package is executable-adjacent pinned TEXT an owner's `.law` module reads.

language "law.core" version "0.2";
package us.irc.temporal version "0.1.1";
namespace "urn:us:irc:temporal:clir";
